Every other series in this project assumes something quietly: that you don't collapse when life hits. The training block, the financial coordinate, the cognitive stack, the regime: all of it is built on the bet that you stay standing long enough to compound. This article is about the thing that actually keeps you standing. It isn't discipline. It's other people. A support system is the structure that holds your order in place, and that is why it gets its own series.

## A support system is not a soft topic

People file relationships under "soft" and money under "hard." That has it backwards. The hard fact is that almost nobody holds their order alone. You can have the perfect protocol and lose it inside a month of a personal crisis if there is no one to catch you. The support system is what makes the rest of the system *robust* rather than brittle.

Think of it the way the name suggests. A stronghold is what you fall back to when the open field turns against you. It does not make you weaker that you need one. It is the reason you can take risks everywhere else: you can quit the job, run the cycle, make the bet, because you know the floor underneath you is held by people who will not let you hit the ground.

> [!important] The order rests on the stronghold
> Discipline keeps your order running on a good day. Your support system keeps it from collapsing on a bad one. The second job is the harder one, and it is the one you cannot do by yourself.

## The real scoreboard question

This series sits inside a bigger idea (the [[wealth-series-architecture|five types of wealth]]): your real scoreboard is not one number but five, and Social Wealth is one of them. Each wealth has a question that cuts through the noise. For Social Wealth, the question is the one nobody wants to sit with:

==Who will be sitting in the front row at your funeral?==

Not your follower count. Not the size of your contact list. The front row is a brutal, accurate test, because it cannot be bought and it cannot be faked. The people there are there because of who you were *to them*, over years, when it cost you something. Everything in this series is in service of that one row.

## Network is your net worth, and why that framing is a trap

You have heard it: *your network is your net worth.* There is truth in it. The people around you shape your opportunities, your information, your odds. But notice what the phrase does. It prices relationships in the currency of money, and the moment you do that, you start treating people as positions on a balance sheet (assets to acquire, liabilities to cut). That logic builds a contact list. It does not build a stronghold.

The front row at your funeral is not full of high-value contacts. It is full of people you never once invoiced. So the "net worth" framing is the *shallow* version of social wealth (useful for the outer ring, poison for the inner one). The whole skill is knowing which ring you are in.

## The radius rule

Here is the model that organises the rest of the series. You do not run one economy across all your relationships. You run different economies at different distances, and you already know this intuitively:

> With my family, I am a communist. With my close friends, a socialist. At the level of community, a democrat. Further out, a republican. At the widest, a libertarian.

Read it as a rule about *keeping score*. With family, you give according to ability and take according to need, and you never count (you don't bill your mother for dinner). With close friends, you share freely but loosely track that it roughly balances over years. Out at the community level, exchange gets more explicit. At the widest radius, it is nearly pure transaction. Call this **the radius rule**: the closer the ring, the less you keep score, and the failure that wrecks relationships is importing the wrong economy into the wrong ring. Score-keeping that is healthy with a vendor is corrosive with a spouse. Generosity that is right with family is a doormat strategy with strangers.

Most relationship pain is a radius error. You are running the wrong economy at the wrong distance.

## The three components

Sahil Bloom splits Social Wealth into three parts, and they map cleanly onto the rings. This series spends one article on each:

- **Depth** ([[Stronghold Part 2.0 - Depth|Part 2.0]]). The small circle of deep, meaningful, loving bonds. The innermost ring, where you keep no score. This is the front row.
- **Breadth** ([[Stronghold Part 3.0 - Breadth|Part 3.0]]). The larger circle of support and belonging beyond yourself: loose ties you learn from, plus connection to something bigger (community, religious, spiritual, cultural). The middle and outer rings.
- **Esteem** ([[Stronghold Part 4.0 - Esteem|Part 4.0]]). The lasting respect, admiration, and trust of your peers, earned through who you are rather than what you bought. The reputation that holds across every ring.

Depth is who catches you. Breadth is who carries you. Esteem is what you are worth to both when you are not in the room.

## Score yourself first

Before you build anything, get a baseline. The way you once checked your net worth, check your Social Wealth. Five statements. Respond to each with **0** (strongly disagree), **1** (disagree), **2** (neutral), **3** (agree), or **4** (strongly agree). Maximum 20.

1. I have a core set of deep, loving, supportive relationships.
2. I am consistently able to be the partner, parent, family member, and friend that I would want to have.
3. I have a network of loose relationships I can learn from and build on.
4. I have a deep feeling of connection to a community (local, regional, national, spiritual, and so on) or to something bigger than myself.
5. I do not attempt to achieve status, respect, or admiration through material purchases.

Add them up. That number is your baseline, and you will come back to it. Notice how the statements group: **1 and 2 are Depth, 3 and 4 are Breadth, 5 is Esteem.** Wherever your lowest pair sits is the article to read most carefully.

Two people, two strongholds. Do not compare your 11 to someone else's 16. Compare it to your own next quarter.

